Yes. (A samurai could also start with ordinary arrows.) What I wanted
to express is; there are five(!) types of arrows, and all are mostly
just useful if you use (if you can use) a bow effectively. Usefulness
of gems are not that class dependent. I use projectiles also to ID
sickness, but in this case as well I don't need so many different
arrow types.

The point is; in my early Nethack days I didn't knew about all the
possible and multifold application of objects and considered many
objects of little usefulness, while with increasing knowledge I just
make use of all the objects properties in the situations where they
are helpful. In early days I just collected but never applied gems,
I never threw a molotov cocktail, never done alchemy, never donated,
etc. Gems now have an application in my games, more than, eg., arrows.

Nethack, I think, is also about ignoring useful objects - and I've
observed power ascenders at NAO that even ignore offensive wands -,
but one should know about (and apply) the options Nethack offers; gems
are just one class of objects in a list of many, to use or to ignore,
depending on the circumstances.

There are only few items in Nethack where I don't see any helpful use.

To seize the suggestion upthread; it would be easy to make damage done
by thrown gems depending on its hardness, say, every gem between 7 and
10 could get some damage bonus, but to me that feels not quite right;
it's more the mass that damages (and that is already implemented).
Uncut diamonds, the objects I expect to find in dungeons, shouldn't do
specific damage.

Would gems become more interesting if usable as slightly more damaging
projectiles? Hardly (I suspect). To make damage, the priority is still
the artifact weapons that you often ackquire even before you got your
first hard gem.

